# Git workflow

This standing instruction is the ask. Do not wait for another "commit" or "open a PR."

## Start

Before the first edit:

1. Fetch `origin`. If this folder is on a clean `main`, pull.
2. If this change already has a branch, stay on it. Otherwise branch from `origin/main`. Name it `addon/<name>/<short>`, `tkmm/<short>`, `web/<short>`, or `fix/<short>`.
3. Edit on that branch.

A question or a plan does not get a branch. A change that spans turns stays on its branch. Unrelated addon, site, and app work each get their own branch.

If the folder has uncommitted work that is not this change, stop. Do not switch off a branch that has uncommitted work.

## Ship

When the change is done:

1. Commit on the branch. The subject is imperative and says why, in one or two sentences: `Sell grey junk in one vendor pass.`
2. Rebase onto `origin/main` when the branch is behind. If the rebase conflicts, stop and name the files.
3. Push, open a pull request, and squash-merge it. The title matches the commit. The body says what a player or the app can do now, and how to check it.
4. Check out `main`, pull, and delete the branch.

Do not merge a change that is unfinished.

## Parallel agents

Two agents do not both edit `Common/`, `Libs/`, `Scripts/`, `TKMM/`, `Web/`, or the same addon. The one that finishes second rebases before merging. An overlap stays on its branch.

## Still ask first

- Do not bump `VERSION` or create a `tkmm-v*` tag unless asked. A player-facing addon change still includes its `RELEASE.md` note, using the version already in `Main.lua`.
- Do not force-push `main`, hard-reset, or skip hooks.
- Leave out secrets, signing keys, `node_modules`, Rust `target`, `dist`, and Terraform state.

`TKMM/` or `Web/` needs `npm install` before you run it. An addon change does not. If the push or merge fails, stop and say what blocked it.
